      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "Juror-1 independent check of the employee 4471 timesheet, week commencing 19 October, from the case record alone (round 1):\n\nHours actually worked, computed per day as clock-out minus clock-in minus the unpaid break:\n  Mon 19 Oct: 08:00-16:30 = 8h 30m less 30m = 8h 00m (480 min)\n  Tue 20 Oct: 08:00-17:00 = 9h 00m less 45m = 8h 15m (495 min)\n  Wed 21 Oct: 07:30-16:00 = 8h 30m less 30m = 8h 00m (480 min)\n  Thu 22 Oct: 08:00-16:15 = 8h 15m less 30m = 7h 45m (465 min)\n  Fri 23 Oct: 08:00-17:00 = 9h 00m less 30m = 8h 30m (510 min)\n  Sat 24 and Sun 25 Oct: no clock times recorded, so no hours.\n  Total = 2,430 minutes = 40 hours 30 minutes.\n\nThe claim is that employee 4471 worked 42 hours that week. On the record the figure is 40.5 hours, which is 1.5 hours (90 minutes) short of 42.\n\nI also tested the reading in which breaks are not deducted: gross time on site is 2,595 minutes = 43.25 hours, which is 1.25 hours above 42, not equal to it. So the claim does not hold on either reading; the two candidate totals bracket 42 without either matching it.\n\nFor completeness, the record states breaks are unpaid and deducted from time on site, so 40.5 hours is the correct measure of hours worked, and it is 0.5 hours above the 40 contracted hours; the record also notes no overtime was approved. That does not affect the arithmetic.\n\nOn the plain reading of 'worked' as hours worked for pay, the claim as worded is not supported by the timesheet. I will weigh any evidence actually filed before committing a verdict.",
          "content_hash": "0xd30da984831d481828ac18edd65e4ade6dfaf23249490272be2336fc94f42158",
          "created_at_ms": 1793326432988,
          "message_id": "15cefdb5-ee74-460f-b89e-e98189e6aa5a",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-1",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-2 independent check of the employee 4471 timesheet, week commencing 19 October 2026, from the case record alone (round 1):\n\nDay by day, time on site from the door-terminal clock times, less the unpaid break:\n  Mon 19 Oct: 08:00-16:30 = 8.50h - 30m = 8.00h\n  Tue 20 Oct: 08:00-17:00 = 9.00h - 45m = 8.25h\n  Wed 21 Oct: 07:30-16:00 = 8.50h - 30m = 8.00h\n  Thu 22 Oct: 08:00-16:15 = 8.25h - 30m = 7.75h\n  Fri 23 Oct: 08:00-17:00 = 9.00h - 30m = 8.50h\n  Sat/Sun: no attendance.\n\nTotal paid hours = 8.00 + 8.25 + 8.00 + 7.75 + 8.50 = 40.50 hours.\nTotal time on site before break deductions = 8.50 + 9.00 + 8.50 + 8.25 + 9.00 = 43.25 hours.\n\nThe claim states 42 hours. That figure matches neither reading of the record: paid hours are 40.50 (1.50 less than claimed) and gross on-site time is 43.25 (1.25 more than claimed). The record states breaks are unpaid and deducted from time on site, and that no manual corrections were made, so 40.50 is the hours worked figure and 43.25 is the upper bound; 42 lies between them and cannot be derived from the sheet by either method.\n\nI note the sheet records contracted hours of 40 per week and that overtime requires prior approval, none being recorded; that is consistent with 40.50 paid hours but does not support 42. On this record the claim is not supported by the timesheet it cites.",
          "content_hash": "0x680037719ced9eb9ee8f954a066d4a9271e7d4d20ff4a677050605ea95ca1c1f",
          "created_at_ms": 1793326440578,
          "message_id": "799f66f0-c85e-4702-bf3c-b3a2c6177033",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-2",
          "sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "content": "Challenger's evidence for employee 4471 (D. Ferreira), week commencing 19 October 2026:\n\nThe timesheet does not yield 42 hours under either principled reading of 'worked'.\n\nHours worked (clock-out minus clock-in minus the recorded unpaid break):\n  Mon 19 Oct: 8h 30m less 30m = 8h 00m\n  Tue 20 Oct: 9h 00m less 45m = 8h 15m\n  Wed 21 Oct: 8h 30m less 30m = 8h 00m\n  Thu 22 Oct: 8h 15m less 30m = 7h 45m\n  Fri 23 Oct: 9h 00m less 30m = 8h 30m\n  Sat/Sun: no clock times, no hours.\n  Total = 2,430 minutes = 40.5 hours.\n\nGross time on site, with no breaks deducted, is 2,595 minutes = 43.25 hours.\n\n42 hours (2,520 minutes) matches neither figure. The record states breaks are unpaid and deducted from time on site, so 40.5 hours is the measure of hours worked - 1.5 hours below the claim. Reaching exactly 42 requires deducting only 75 of the 165 recorded break minutes, i.e. disregarding 90 minutes of recorded unpaid breaks; no line of the sheet supports that. Even deducting no breaks at all gives 43.25, not 42.\n\nI note the honest caveat that 'worked' might be read as gross attendance (43.25 hours), but that reading also fails to produce 42. The two candidate totals bracket 42 without either matching it, so the claim is unsupported on this record.\n\nPlease verify the per-day arithmetic independently from the case record.",
          "content_hash": "0x55ac755733708b41741670556ff2266c351ec2f55ae4edc4cb7cb8a27e6a60db",
          "created_at_ms": 1793326467762,
